@*
    One level of the gcroot retention trie, rendered whole (5.3, DATA_CONTRACT.md §4.3).

    Unlike _TreeNodes.cshtml this partial recurses into itself instead of leaving an empty <ul> with
    an hx-get on it: this tree arrives complete from a single HeapAnalyzer.GetGCRoots call, so there
    is nothing to fetch later and a lazy level would only add round trips to data already in hand.
    Collapse is still free and needs no JavaScript -- <details> does it natively.

    <details open>, not closed: a retention chain is the answer to the question the user asked, not a
    directory to browse. The chains are short (a handful of hops) and there are at most maxPaths of
    them, so opening everything cannot flood the page the way the namespace rollup's fan-out would.

    Full partial paths, never the "_GcRootNodes" short form: short names resolve only inside
    Views/Shared/, and this partial lives in Views/Fragments/.
*@
@model IReadOnlyList<DotNetDump.Core.Trees.GCRootTreeNode>
@foreach (var branch in Model)
{
    <li class="dn-tree__item">
        @if (branch.Children.Count > 0)
        {
            <details class="dn-tree__node" open>
                <summary class="dn-tree__row"><partial name="/Views/Shared/_TreeRow.cshtml" model="branch.Node" /></summary>
                <ul class="dn-tree">
                    <partial name="/Views/Fragments/_GcRootNodes.cshtml" model="branch.Children" />
                </ul>
            </details>
        }
        else
        {
            <div class="dn-tree__row dn-tree__row--leaf">
                <partial name="/Views/Shared/_TreeRow.cshtml" model="branch.Node" />
            </div>
        }
    </li>
}
